{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 5,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B011",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B033",
    "B031"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B033 to B011. Fluctuation theorems remain an exceptionally elegant and broadly tested set of exact relations, but the discussion has made the credit boundary among related formulations a more consequential weakness than I initially assigned it. Optical lattice clocks instead join a clean physical mechanism—magic-wavelength confinement suppressing motional clock shifts—to demanding, independently reproduced experimental performance. Katori's originating concept and Ye's decisive experimental development form a compact attribution, while the clocks are already mature instruments for precision measurement and fundamental tests. This is a case where the essential mechanism, realization, and continuing physical capability remain coherently connected.",
    "case_against": "The citation must be kept strictly to the optical-lattice-clock architecture. It cannot absorb the wider histories of optical frequency standards or frequency-comb technology, and a historical examination is still required to establish that Katori and Ye capture the decisive conceptual and experimental contributions. My change does not imply that B033 has lost scientific depth; rather, B011 currently satisfies the combined requirements of merit, maturity, and exact credit more securely.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth identifies magic-wavelength confinement as the physical advance that made exceptional accuracy achievable and stresses the complementary Katori-Ye attribution and independent reproduction.",
        "response": "I find this formulation decisive. It identifies a simple physical solution to a concrete limitation, rather than relying only on record-setting performance. The replicated experimental realization supplies the maturity that a prize for an experimental architecture should demonstrate."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that lattice clocks have already become robust precision-measurement instruments, whereas quantum error correction, though profound, has not yet delivered comparably mature broadly reproducible capability.",
        "response": "I agree with the distinction. It has also led me to revise my earlier comparison with fluctuation theorems: established physical laws deserve high weight, but the lattice-clock case now presents an unusually complete chain from central insight through realization to a durable measurement capability."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson supports Nd-Fe-B magnets for their mature materials impact but warns that both the equal credit of Sagawa and Croat and the fundamentally physical character of the achievement require resolution.",
        "response": "The magnets remain a compelling materials achievement, but those two reservations are material. B011 has the more sharply defined physical architecture and a clearer present attribution, provided its boundary from enabling optical-clock developments is maintained."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record can delimit optical lattice clocks from preceding optical-frequency-standard and frequency-comb developments without omitting indispensable contributors.",
      "Whether Evans, Jarzynski, and Crooks can be credited in a historically exact way for a citation confined to the central fluctuation-theorem relations."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I share the Gothenburg physics environment with committee colleagues Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson; no collaboration or connection to the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
